course: dictionary
lesson: 7143
title: Many meanings of people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «people» has more than one meaning, so you need its context to know which one is meant. Read the words around it and ask: is this about a few individuals, about everyone, or about a nation?
explain: «Many people work on it: a farmer grows wheat, a miller grinds flour, a baker bakes.» Here people means more than one person. The list shows separate workers, so you can swap in the word persons and it still makes sense.
explain: «Both things can be true: radiation hurts animals, yet people being gone helps them more in many places.» Here people means humans in general. Words like «a people» (with peoples as its plural) mean a nation or group with its own language and ways, and that is a different meaning again.
fact: «People» means more than one person: men, women and children.
fact: «People» can also mean persons in general, or everyone, as in «people say».
fact: A «people» (plural «peoples») is a nation or group with its own language and ways.
fact: Casually, «my people» means your family or friends, and «peeps» is slang for people or friends.
fact: Formal notices and exams use words like «persons», «individuals», «occupants» and «passengers» instead.
exercise: «Many people work on a loaf of bread, from farmer to shopkeeper.» What does people mean here? | My family and friends | Only one single person | A nation with its own language | *More than one person: the farmer, miller, baker and driver
exercise: Maya says, «Me and my people are going to the park.» What does my people mean? | Her country and its language | *Her family or friends | Strangers she has never met | Her teachers at school
exercise: A notice says «a maximum of 40 persons». Which word means the same as persons here? | Macaws | Peeps | Ice | *Individuals
test: «The hyacinth macaw's beak cracks palm nuts so hard people need a hammer.» What does people mean in this sentence? | *Everyone in general | Parrots only | My family and friends | A nation with its own language
test: Which sentence uses people to mean a nation or group with its own language and ways? | Peeps, come over here! | Some people, honestly, never pay. | Loads of people came to the party. | *The peoples of the world speak many different languages.
exam: Which word is the formal word for people on a public notice? | Peeps | Mates | *Persons | Loads
</br>
